rride this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Aaron","term":"Aaron"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Aaron","sourceTitle":"Aaron"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"bread","senseEvidence":{"basis":"source_verse_citation","originalCitations":[{"anchorIndex":25,"href":"?bref=Lev.2.5","rawReference":"Lev.2.5"}],"ruleId":"Bread::Bread::source_verse_citation","sourceHeadwordId":"Bread"},"text":"If your offering is a grain offering prepared on a griddle, it must be unleavened bread made of fine flour mixed with oil.","verse":5}],"id":"Bread","label":"bread","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Bread::Bread::source_verse_citation","scope":"source_verse_citation","senseReview":"Reviewed original headword and source sense. This substantive word is admitted only where the original article explicitly cites the exact occurrence verse; ambiguous homographs and known context mismatches are separately excluded. This is selective contextual evidence, not an automatic rule for every same-spelling word.","sourceHeadwordId":"Bread","term":"Bread"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Bread","sourceTitle":"Bread"},{"evidence":[{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"“When anyone brings a grain offering to the LORD, his offering must consist of fine flour. He is to pour olive oil on it, put frankincense on it,","verse":1},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"and bring it to Aaron’s sons the priests. The priest shall take a handful of the flour and oil, together with all the frankincense, and burn this as a memorial portion on the altar, a food offering, a pleasing aroma to the LORD.","verse":2},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"The remainder of the grain offering shall belong to Aaron and his sons; it is a most holy part of the food offerings to the LORD.","verse":3},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"When you bring to the LORD the grain offering made in any of these ways, it is to be presented to the priest, and he shall take it to the altar.","verse":8},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"The priest is to remove the memorial portion from the grain offering and burn it on the altar as a food offering, a pleasing aroma to the LORD.","verse":9},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"But the remainder of the grain offering shall belong to Aaron and his sons; it is a most holy part of the food offerings to the LORD.","verse":10},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"No grain offering that you present to the LORD may be made with leaven, for you are not to burn any leaven or honey as a food offering to the LORD.","verse":11},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"You may bring them to the LORD as an offering of firstfruits, but they must not go up on the altar as a pleasing aroma.","verse":12},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"If you bring a grain offering of firstfruits to the LORD, you shall offer crushed heads of new grain roasted on the fire.","verse":14},{"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","phrase":"LORD","senseEvidence":{"basis":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","originalCitations":[],"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord"},"text":"The priest shall then burn the memorial portion of the crushed grain and the oil, together with all its frankincense, as a food offering to the LORD.","verse":16}],"id":"Lord","label":"LORD","matchReviews":[{"ruleId":"Lord::LORD::reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","scope":"reviewed_capitalized_divine_title","senseReview":"Only literal uppercase LORD is admitted globally. Source Lord article explicitly covers God as Lord; lowercase human lord and sentence-title Lord are not inferred from this rule.","sourceHeadwordId":"Lord","term":"LORD"}],"matchingRule":"reviewed_exact_headword","matchingRules":["reviewed_exact_headword"],"sourceProvenanceId":"Lord","sourceTitle":"Lord"}],"format":"bsb-study-chapter-v2","notes":[{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Lev.2.1-16","r":"Lev.2.1-16","rawR":"Lev.2.1-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.2.1-16","value":"Lev.2.1-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:1-16"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ",{"c":["grain offering"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (Hebrew ",{"c":["minkhah,"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"}," “gift, present”) was a way to express worship to the Lord through a gift, recognizing him as Lord over the life of the worshiper.  The grain offering was to accompany each whole burnt offering (",{"href":"?bref=Num.28.3-6","r":"Num.28.3-6","rawR":"Num.28.3-6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.28.3-6","value":"Num.28.3-6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 28:3-6"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Num.28.12-13","r":"Num.28.12-13","rawR":"Num.28.12-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.28.12-13","value":"Num.28.12-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"12-13"},"), sin offering (",{"href":"?bref=Num.6.14-15","r":"Num.6.14-15","rawR":"Num.6.14-15","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.6.14-15","value":"Num.6.14-15"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 6:14-15"},"), and peace offering (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.9.4","r":"Lev.9.4","rawR":"Lev.9.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.9.4","value":"Lev.9.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 9:4"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Num.6.17","r":"Num.6.17","rawR":"Num.6.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.6.17","value":"Num.6.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 6:17"},"). The grain offering supplied the priest with bread for his daily sustenance, because no farmland was allocated to priests."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Lev.2.1-16","refs":[{"book":"LEV","endChapter":2,"endVerse":16,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Lev.2.1-16","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"LEV","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"b4e748ecc754f156f9e2b2c9d183100caa957f5a2e1c395db1914e1ec2108ce5","sourceItemName":"Lev.2.1-16","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1250,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"LEV","canonicalOSIS":"Lev","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Lev"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Lev.2.1-16","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Lev.2.1-16","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Lev.2.1","r":"Lev.2.1","rawR":"Lev.2.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.2.1","value":"Lev.2.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:1"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["Choice flour"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was fine wheat flour (in contrast with common barley flour). See also ",{"href":"?bref=1Kgs.4.22","r":"1Kgs.4.22","rawR":"1Kgs.4.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Kgs.4.22","value":"1Kgs.4.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Kgs 4:22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Ezek.16.13","r":"Ezek.16.13","rawR":"Ezek.16.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezek.16.13","value":"Ezek.16.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ezek 16:13"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Ezek.16.19","r":"Ezek.16.19","rawR":"Ezek.16.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezek.16.19","value":"Ezek.16.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"19"},". • Israelites used ",{"c":["olive oil"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," as shortening in their cooking, as lamp fuel, and for medicinal and cosmetic purposes. Accordingly, it was associated with the rich produce of the land and with God’s provision (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.8.8","r":"Deut.8.8","rawR":"Deut.8.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.8.8","value":"Deut.8.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 8:8"},"). • Like choice flour, ",{"c":["frankincense"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was frequently connected with the Tabernacle (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.30.34","r":"Exod.30.34","rawR":"Exod.30.34","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.30.34","value":"Exod.30.34"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 30:34"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.24.7","r":"Lev.24.7","rawR":"Lev.24.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.24.7","value":"Lev.24.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 24:7"},") or the Temple (",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.9.29","r":"1Chr.9.29","rawR":"1Chr.9.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.9.29","value":"1Chr.9.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 9:29"},"). This fragrant tree resin was also used by individuals on special occasions (",{"href":"?bref=Song.3.6","r":"Song.3.6","rawR":"Song.3.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Song.3.6","value":"Song.3.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Song 3:6"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.2.11","r":"Matt.2.11","rawR":"Matt.2.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.2.11","value":"Matt.2.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 2:11"},"). It was a primary ingredient of the holy incense burned in the Holy Place as a tangible expression of worship (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.30.34-38","r":"Exod.30.34-38","rawR":"Exod.30.34-38","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.30.34-38","value":"Exod.30.34-38"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 30:34-38"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Lev.2.1","refs":[{"book":"LEV","endChapter":2,"endVerse":1,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":1}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Lev.2.1","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"LEV","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"cad866c193ac30200168b6f2c766130c5cd23560c74d79b002742f0fe66af7d4","sourceItemName":"Lev.2.1","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1251,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"LEV","canonicalOSIS":"Lev","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Lev"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Lev.2.1","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Lev.2.1","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Lev.2.2","r":"Lev.2.2","rawR":"Lev.2.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.2.2","value":"Lev.2.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:2"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," The ",{"c":["representative portion"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," of the grain offering was burned on the altar to remind the worshiper that the whole offering belonged to God."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Lev.2.2","refs":[{"book":"LEV","endChapter":2,"endVerse":2,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":2}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Lev.2.2","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"LEV","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"eb3a06af0dfa388420d4e6086b13bcd214c1dd465b2b808f228c02abe028e40a","sourceItemName":"Lev.2.2","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1252,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"LEV","canonicalOSIS":"Lev","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Lev"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Lev.2.2","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Lev.2.2","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Lev.2.3","r":"Lev.2.3","rawR":"Lev.2.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.2.3","value":"Lev.2.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:3"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["rest of the grain offering:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," A portion of the fire offerings was generally reserved for the priest as food (see ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.6.16-17","r":"Lev.6.16-17","rawR":"Lev.6.16-17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.6.16-17","value":"Lev.6.16-17"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:16-17"},"). Exceptions included the whole burnt offering (the priest received only the hide; see ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.7.8","r":"Lev.7.8","rawR":"Lev.7.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.7.8","value":"Lev.7.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:8"},"), offerings given by the priest for himself (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.4.3-12","r":"Lev.4.3-12","rawR":"Lev.4.3-12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.4.3-12","value":"Lev.4.3-12"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:3-12"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.6.19-23","r":"Lev.6.19-23","rawR":"Lev.6.19-23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.6.19-23","value":"Lev.6.19-23"}],"t":"ref","v":"6:19-23"},"), or offerings made for the entire community (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.4.13-21","r":"Lev.4.13-21","rawR":"Lev.4.13-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.4.13-21","value":"Lev.4.13-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"4:13-21"},"). In these cases, the offering was to be entirely burned. The grain offering provided the priests with bread for their diet. • ",{"c":["most holy"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," (Hebrew ",{"c":["qodesh qodashim"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"},{"c":[","],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," “holy of holies”): Both God and what belongs to him are holy. “Most holy” designates the priestly share as taken from the holy offering and reserved for a special function. This description is used for the priest’s share of any gifts dedicated to the Lord (",{"href":"?bref=Num.18.8-10","r":"Num.18.8-10","rawR":"Num.18.8-10","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.18.8-10","value":"Num.18.8-10"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 18:8-10"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Lev.2.3","refs":[{"book":"LEV","endChapter":2,"endVerse":3,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":3}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Lev.2.3","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"LEV","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"0a438fed6caead11ff86f4628b610ff7999f0d28818db0eb7a92b94dc760e154","sourceItemName":"Lev.2.3","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1253,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"LEV","canonicalOSIS":"Lev","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Lev"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Lev.2.3","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Lev.2.3","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Lev.2.4","r":"Lev.2.4","rawR":"Lev.2.4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.2.4","value":"Lev.2.4"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:4"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," A large ceramic ",{"c":["oven"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was placed in a hole, and around it a fire was kindled. Once the oven was hot, the flat bread baked against the inside of its wall. • ",{"c":["without any yeast:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," See ",{"href":"?item=Lev.2.11_StudyNote_Filament","id":"Lev.2.11_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"Lev.2.11_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"study note on 2:11"},"; see also ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.12.8","r":"Exod.12.8","rawR":"Exod.12.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.12.8","value":"Exod.12.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 12:8"},"."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Lev.2.4","refs":[{"book":"LEV","endChapter":2,"endVerse":4,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":4}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Lev.2.4","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"LEV","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"13a7c3dbe6e3cf91d2987ff2b8e36bc3ee50c0049c9966e4ede76d9c510abf42","sourceItemName":"Lev.2.4","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1254,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"LEV","canonicalOSIS":"Lev","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Lev"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Lev.2.4","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Lev.2.4","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Lev.2.5","r":"Lev.2.5","rawR":"Lev.2.5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.2.5","value":"Lev.2.5"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:5"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," A ",{"c":["griddle"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was a flat stone or clay surface propped above a fire and heated. Later griddles were made of metal (",{"href":"?bref=Ezek.4.3","r":"Ezek.4.3","rawR":"Ezek.4.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezek.4.3","value":"Ezek.4.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ezek 4:3"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Lev.2.5","refs":[{"book":"LEV","endChapter":2,"endVerse":5,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":5}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Lev.2.5","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"LEV","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"5bed35031a05f3e91f01a381f76bff8dc84d2f2852cdc2e11416a272a6fa5fa6","sourceItemName":"Lev.2.5","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1255,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"LEV","canonicalOSIS":"Lev","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Lev"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Lev.2.5","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Lev.2.5","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Lev.2.7","r":"Lev.2.7","rawR":"Lev.2.7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.2.7","value":"Lev.2.7"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:7"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," Dough ",{"c":["prepared in a pan"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," was fried with ",{"c":["oil"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," until crisp."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Lev.2.7","refs":[{"book":"LEV","endChapter":2,"endVerse":7,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":7}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Lev.2.7","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"LEV","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"76c6af2c484dc62d627eb4dec06238d23881c1f60b4421aa91fc823099dbf54c","sourceItemName":"Lev.2.7","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1256,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"LEV","canonicalOSIS":"Lev","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Lev"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Lev.2.7","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Lev.2.7","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Lev.2.11","r":"Lev.2.11","rawR":"Lev.2.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.2.11","value":"Lev.2.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:11"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["yeast:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The bread commonly eaten in biblical times was comparable to modern sourdough. The substance that is often called “leaven” consisted of wild yeast spores mixed into moist dough or a flour mixture. The yeast would grow and cause the dough to ferment, souring and raising it. A reserved portion of the sour dough would be kneaded into subsequent batches (",{"href":"?bref=Matt.13.33","r":"Matt.13.33","rawR":"Matt.13.33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.13.33","value":"Matt.13.33"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 13:33"},"). Unleavened bread was required for the Passover (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.12.8","r":"Exod.12.8","rawR":"Exod.12.8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.12.8","value":"Exod.12.8"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 12:8"},"), in remembrance of Israel’s bondage in Egypt (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.16.3","r":"Deut.16.3","rawR":"Deut.16.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.16.3","value":"Deut.16.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 16:3"},") and their speedy departure from that land (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.12.34","r":"Exod.12.34","rawR":"Exod.12.34","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.12.34","value":"Exod.12.34"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 12:34"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.13.3","r":"Exod.13.3","rawR":"Exod.13.3","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.13.3","value":"Exod.13.3"}],"t":"ref","v":"13:3"},"). Following the Exodus, unleavened bread acquired the status of altar bread. Leavened bread was not to be burned on the altar. • ",{"c":["honey:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," The Hebrew word (",{"c":["debash"],"sourceClass":"hebrew","t":"span"},") can also refer to fruit nectar. The reason for its exclusion is not stated, but perhaps the frequent use of honey in pagan offerings, particularly to gods of the underworld, was a contributing reason. If the word refers to fruit nectar, its association with wine and fermentation may have linked it with yeast, making it ineligible to be burned on the altar. However, both honey and yeast were allowed as part of an offering of first crops (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.2.12","r":"Lev.2.12","rawR":"Lev.2.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.2.12","value":"Lev.2.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 2:12"},"). Loaves of bread made with yeast were to be presented with thank offerings (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.7.13","r":"Lev.7.13","rawR":"Lev.7.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.7.13","value":"Lev.7.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"7:13"},") and as a gift to the priest in the Festival of Harvest (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.23.17","r":"Lev.23.17","rawR":"Lev.23.17","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.23.17","value":"Lev.23.17"}],"t":"ref","v":"23:17"},"). In the New Testament, yeast becomes a spiritual metaphor, playing on its sour taste and on its tendency to spread (",{"href":"?bref=Matt.16.6","r":"Matt.16.6","rawR":"Matt.16.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.16.6","value":"Matt.16.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 16:6"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.16.11","r":"Matt.16.11","rawR":"Matt.16.11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.16.11","value":"Matt.16.11"}],"t":"ref","v":"11"},", ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.16.12","r":"Matt.16.12","rawR":"Matt.16.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.16.12","value":"Matt.16.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"12"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=1Cor.5.6-8","r":"1Cor.5.6-8","rawR":"1Cor.5.6-8","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Cor.5.6-8","value":"1Cor.5.6-8"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Cor 5:6-8"},"; cp. ",{"href":"?bref=Matt.13.33","r":"Matt.13.33","rawR":"Matt.13.33","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Matt.13.33","value":"Matt.13.33"}],"t":"ref","v":"Matt 13:33"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Lev.2.11","refs":[{"book":"LEV","endChapter":2,"endVerse":11,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":11}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Lev.2.11","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"LEV","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"f4ac5a0d91a2fcdbabe8d458e77fab90691a709bbe484709d8b94e9f45b33905","sourceItemName":"Lev.2.11","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1257,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"LEV","canonicalOSIS":"Lev","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Lev"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Lev.2.11","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Lev.2.11","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Lev.2.12","r":"Lev.2.12","rawR":"Lev.2.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.2.12","value":"Lev.2.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:12"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," ",{"c":["offering of the first crops:"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," This offering (called “firstfruits” in many translations) was brought from the first part of the harvest (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.23.15-21","r":"Lev.23.15-21","rawR":"Lev.23.15-21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.23.15-21","value":"Lev.23.15-21"}],"t":"ref","v":"23:15-21"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Num.28.26-31","r":"Num.28.26-31","rawR":"Num.28.26-31","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.28.26-31","value":"Num.28.26-31"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 28:26-31"},"). Like the representative portion of the grain offering, this offering acknowledged that the entire harvest belonged to God (",{"href":"?bref=Lev.23.10-11","r":"Lev.23.10-11","rawR":"Lev.23.10-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.23.10-11","value":"Lev.23.10-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 23:10-11"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.22.29","r":"Exod.22.29","rawR":"Exod.22.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.22.29","value":"Exod.22.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"Exod 22:29"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.23.19","r":"Exod.23.19","rawR":"Exod.23.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.23.19","value":"Exod.23.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"23:19"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Exod.34.26","r":"Exod.34.26","rawR":"Exod.34.26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.34.26","value":"Exod.34.26"}],"t":"ref","v":"34:26"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Num.15.18-20","r":"Num.15.18-20","rawR":"Num.15.18-20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.15.18-20","value":"Num.15.18-20"}],"t":"ref","v":"Num 15:18-20"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.18.4-5","r":"Deut.18.4-5","rawR":"Deut.18.4-5","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.18.4-5","value":"Deut.18.4-5"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deut 18:4-5"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.26.1-2","r":"Deut.26.1-2","rawR":"Deut.26.1-2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.26.1-2","value":"Deut.26.1-2"}],"t":"ref","v":"26:1-2"},"). It further recognized that the land itself, not just its products, belonged to God; Israel was merely the tenant (see ",{"href":"?bref=Lev.25.23","r":"Lev.25.23","rawR":"Lev.25.23","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.25.23","value":"Lev.25.23"}],"t":"ref","v":"Lev 25:23"},")."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Lev.2.12","refs":[{"book":"LEV","endChapter":2,"endVerse":12,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":12}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Lev.2.12","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"LEV","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"11b7b4010d2a3cdf3fd93cb32ceecaa44f952db462e8c4d7d23953119836cc07","sourceItemName":"Lev.2.12","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1258,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"LEV","canonicalOSIS":"Lev","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Lev"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Lev.2.12","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Lev.2.12","type":"StudyNote"},{"chapterAssociationStatus":"canonical_reference","content":[{"c":[{"c":[{"href":"?bref=Lev.2.13","r":"Lev.2.13","rawR":"Lev.2.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Lev.2.13","value":"Lev.2.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"2:13"}],"sourceClass":"sn-ref","t":"span"}," In ancient times, ",{"c":["salt"],"sourceClass":"sn-excerpt","t":"span"}," represented permanence because it preserved food. A covenant of salt (see study notes on ",{"href":"?item=Num.18.19_StudyNote_Filament","id":"Num.18.19_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"Num.18.19_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"Num 18:19"},"; ",{"href":"?item=IIChr.13.5_StudyNote_Filament","id":"IIChr.13.5_StudyNote_Filament","rawId":"IIChr.13.5_StudyNote_Filament","t":"link","v":"2 Chr 13:5"},") denoted an enduring covenant that was renewed with successive generations."],"sourceClass":"sn-text","t":"p"}],"id":"StudyNote:Lev.2.13","refs":[{"book":"LEV","endChapter":2,"endVerse":13,"startChapter":2,"startVerse":13}],"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Lev.2.13","product":"TyndaleOpenStudyNotes","typename":"StudyNote"},"sourceBookAppCode":"LEV","sourceChapterCoverage":[2],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"ed9d40d379f60999421aaee85bad7307e90cfaba3c9f43b9a3aadb5ae8fe1d4c","sourceItemName":"Lev.2.13","sourceMember":"Tyndale Open Study Notes/StudyNotes.xml","sourceOrder":1259,"sourceReferenceException":false,"sourceReferenceMappingEvidence":{"appCode":"LEV","canonicalOSIS":"Lev","method":"official_book_intro_ref_title","sourceOSIS":"Lev"},"sourceReferenceStatus":"canonical","sourceRefsExact":"Lev.2.13","sourceRelease":"1.25","sourceTitleExact":null,"supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[],"title":"Lev.2.13","type":"StudyNote"}],"version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}